{"ts": 1789783368.702729, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This is informative. Adding a weak/noise feature to {13,39} actually *hurts* relative to the strong triples (0.86\u20131.01 vs 1.06\u20131.09), and often dilutes below the 0.931 pair. That's the signature of real signal: the top features contribute genuine information, while weak ones add variance without mean. If I were merely noise-mining, I'd expect the weak-feature triples to occasionally match the strong ones.\n\nI'll take {13, 39, 33}, all long. Now the key question is what to predict out-of-sample \u2014 and the honest answer is meaningfully below 1.089.\n\nThree distinct sources of shrinkage apply:\n\n**Selection bias.** I evaluated 40 singles, 16 pairs, and 22 triples \u2014 roughly 78 specifications. Choosing the maximum of many noisy estimates guarantees the winner's in-sample value is upward-biased: whichever spec landed on top did so partly because its noise draw was favorable. With ~0.22 SE per estimate and a tight cluster of near-ties at the top, the winner's-curse correction is on the order of 0.15\u20130.25.\n\n**The near-tie cluster is the tell.** Five different triples fall within 0.05 of each other. That spacing is well inside the noise band, meaning the ranking among them is largely arbitrary. The expected OOS value of \"the one that happened to win\" is closer to the *cluster average* (~1.05) than to the observed max, before further shrinkage.\n\n**Genuine underlying signal.** Against those deflators, the structural evidence is real: sign inversion maps cleanly to \u22121.089, single flips degrade sharply, features combine near-orthogonally with \u221an scaling, and weak features fail to substitute for strong ones. This isn't a pure overfit \u2014 there is true signal, likely supporting a Sharpe around 0.85\u20130.95.\n\nNetting these out, I center my belief near 0.88 with a fairly wide standard deviation. The SD must cover not just estimation error (~0.22) but regime shift and the possibility that some of the feature strength is period-specific \u2014 so I widen to 0.30."}
