{"ts": 1789783406.198668, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Specification:** Features 13, 39, 33 \u2014 all long, equal weight.\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 1.089\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.88 \u00b1 0.30\n\n### How I got there\n\nI screened all 40 features individually, then explored pairs and triples among the leaders. Seven features stood out as long signals: 13 (0.68), 39 (0.63), 33 (0.58), 25 (0.55), 38 (0.50), 20 (0.43), 26 (0.40).\n\n### Why I believe this is real signal\n\nSeveral checks pointed the same direction:\n\n- **Sign inversion is exact.** Flipping all three signs yields precisely \u22121.089, and each individual flip degrades performance sharply (to 0.29\u20130.43). The orientation is structural, not fitted.\n- **Near-orthogonal combination.** Pairs and triples scale close to \u221an of their components, indicating the features carry complementary rather than redundant information.\n- **Weak features don't substitute.** Swapping a marginal feature into the strong pair *lowered* Sharpe (0.86\u20131.01) versus strong triples (1.06\u20131.09). Under pure noise-mining, weak features would sometimes match the strong ones. They didn't.\n\n### Why my prediction sits well below 1.089\n\nWith n=5000, the standard error on any single Sharpe estimate is about 0.22 (\u221a(252/5000)). Two adjustments follow:\n\n**Winner's curse.** I tested ~78 specifications. The one that ranked highest did so partly on a favorable noise draw, so its in-sample figure is upward-biased by roughly 0.15\u20130.25.\n\n**The top is a statistical tie.** Five triples fall within 0.05 of one another \u2014 far inside the noise band. The ranking among them is close to arbitrary, so the fair anchor is the cluster average (~1.05), not the observed maximum.\n\nDiscounting 1.089 for selection bias while crediting the genuine structural evidence puts my central estimate at 0.88. I set the SD at 0.30 rather than the bare 0.22 estimation error, since OOS data also carries regime-shift risk and the chance that some feature strength is specific to this sample period.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: every diagnostic above is in-sample. The sign and orthogonality checks confirm internal consistency, but none of them can rule out that these features were jointly strong in this particular period for reasons that won't persist."}
